
'This script will be stripped from StarSac2.s2s, and possibly other scripts like ConteTaskSaccade.s2s as well.
'Here we are implementing the "Chicago" task, whose introduction has been delayed as we get the Purkinje eyetracker
'online.  The idea of the Chicago task is that following a central fixation, the animal will be cued to saccade to
'a peripheral target, but *during the saccade* to said target, the target will be removed and placed in a second
'location, and the animal will have to saccade to the second location for reward.  This means that we need to be
'able to quickly detect a saccade - though I believe that a simple central fixation break should be enough.  If the
'animal breaks central fixation and does *not* end up in the intended first-saccade location by a particular time, 
'then the trial should be aborted.  Presumably a minimal stay in the first-saccade location will be required (long 
'enough to plan the second saccade) and then a saccade to the second location should be rewarded.

'OK, the plan here is being altered (pray I do not alter it again).  The ORIGINAL design of this task presented a
'T1 target, waited for the animal to saccade to the T1 target, and then during the saccade removed the T1 target 
'and replaced it with a T2 target.  The idea was that a manipulation of an efference copy signal (in this case
'from V1 into the pulvinar) would cause the animal's internal representation of gaze direction to be faulty on
'account of the manipulated efference copy, and that this faulty internal representation would result in a change
'in the saccade to T2.  This was based on a prior result where manipulation of an efference copy signal from SC
'going to FEF (manipulated in the intermediate location of medial dorsal thalamus) brought about just such an
'effect.  However, one very big difference between the planned design and the design of the SC-MD-FEF task was
'that in the SC-MD-FEF task the T1 and T2 targets were BOTH presented during fixation and both were removed prior
'to the double saccade.  This made the task in effect a working memory task, where the presumption is that a
'faulty internal representation of gaze direction at T1 (in fact the first saccade was completed correctly) 
'resulted in the second saccade attempting to compensate for a perceived undershoot in the internal representation.
'The problem that I identified is that under our original design, the animal WOULD NOT KNOW where the T2 was, and
'therefore where to direct the second saccade, until after arriving at the T1 point.  At that point, my intuition
'is that it does not matter at all whether the internal representation is faulty, because the T2 saccade vector
'must be calculated from the current eye position, whether the internal representation of that eye position is
'faulty or not.  As an example, assume fixation is at (0,0).  The T1 is placed at (5,0).  When the animal saccades
'to (5,0) we manipulate the efference copy of the saccade; let's assume that the internal representation places
'the end of the first saccade at (3,0) instead of (5,0).  Now, we place the T2 at (5,5) - the correct saccade is
'five degrees up.  If the animal's internal representation is correct, they will saccade to (5,5).  But if the
'animal's internal representation tells it that its gaze direction is at (3,0), then the animal will believe that
'the T2 is at (3,5) and the correct saccade is...five degrees up.  Same saccade.  It's the working memory component
'of the task that makes this manipulation work.  The animal plans a saccade to (5,0) and then a second saccade to
'(5,5).  When the first saccade completes and the animal believes it has ended up at (3,0) - even though it is 
'actaully at (5,0) - it should compensate on the second saccade by adding two degrees of rightward saccade to try
'to reach the second target.  (Of course, this only works if the system that the animal uses to represent the
'double saccade is "Location 1"/"Location 2"...if the animal uses "Saccade vector 1"/"Saccade vector 2" then the 
'animal would presumably have little reason to refer to the internal representation of gaze direction!)
'
'Long story short, we don't need to perform the difficult task of dropping the T1 during the saccade, instead we
'need to make sure the double saccade is planned before stimulus presentation.  The tricky question now is WHEN
'TO PERFORM THE JAWS MANIPULATION?  The SC-MD-FEF manipulation would appropriately be done during the first saccade
'to damage the efference copy of the saccade (generated from SC).  But note that in that experiment they used
'muscimol, so there was no temporal precision of the block.  But if we are blocking V1 efference copy through
'pulvinar, there doesn't seem to be any reason that V1 would be active during the saccade, because the T1 (which
'is to be located in the JAWS field) will not be present in the JF during that time.  So what would V1 be signaling?
'We presumably want to block during the T1 presentation...which won't block saccade information, so what will it be
'blocking?  This is set up for failure.

'The task has changed ever so slightly, and so let me roll it out yet again:
'
'1) Fixation up
'2*) Acquire fixation
'    Hold time
'3*) T1 Up - T1 will be a grating (orientation from recorded neuron)
'    Delay time
'4*) T2a and T2b up - these will be gratings, one with the same orientation as T1, the other orthogonal.
'    Delay time
'5*) All Ts down (but fixation still up)
'    Delay time
'6*) Fixation down (saccade to T1 allowed)
'    Wait saccade T1 and hold
'7*) Saccade T1 complete
'    Wait saccade T2 and hold
'8) Saccade T2 complete
'9) Reward if correct (what is reward case for JAWS?  The saccade may miss!  IMPORTANT POINT)
'
'Stars indicate periods during which JAWS would be allowable.
'
'We can totally just do a drop-down for JAWS start and JAWS end with start options of "None", "Fixation Acquired",
'"Targets Up", "Targets Down", "Saccade 1 Allowed", "Saccade 1 Complete", then another drop down for JAWS off starting
'with "None" and offset 1 including "Saccade 2 Complete".  Obviously most of these would never be used, but it's easy
'enough to keep things flexible.  BUT we do have to think about the fact that we don't want to have the JAWS on for
'too long, I think Henry would prefer 500 ms or less per trial, so that would make four straight states not really
'plausible.  Probably only during state 3, unless 2 is short.  Even then, maybe don't want to JAWS on a trial when the
'animal might not actually start the trial.
'
'So T1 is in the JAWS field - not clear whether it is jittering.
'T2a and T2b need to be in the same hemifield, I am going to insist opposite quadrant
'Marty says that all four positions (X,Y for each T2) are to be set independently by the experimenter
'We have to be able to mirror those as well - that mirror is going to be L/R, and I don't want to hear any objection

'Another thing, we have to have a training pathway.  Should look something like this:
'Assume we can start the movie with S (I don't recall if that's the letter)
'The basic scheme would go:
'"F", acquisition, wait1, "S", (wait2, "f",) saccade1, (wait3, saccade2,) "X"
'I think that would work for anything.
'Fix Up - T1 Up - Allow Saccade1   First step allows saccade ASAT, skips both optional paths 
'Fix Up - T1 Up - Fix Down - Allow Saccade1   Next step requires hold of WAIT2 (start at 0, increase), allows saccade once fix is down
'                                            Here T1 Up (movie duration) is longer than WAIT2
'Fix Up - T1 Up - Fix Down - Allow Saccade1 - T2 Up After Saccade1 - Allow Saccade2   Maybe this is where we introduce the T2 (not all trials)
'Fix Up - T1 Up - Fix Down - T2 Up And Allow Saccade1 - Allow Saccade2
'Fix Up - T1 Up - T2 Up - Fix Down - Allow Saccade1 - Allow Saccade2
'Fix Up - T1 Up - T2 Up - Fix Down - Allow Saccade1 - T1/T2 Down - Allow Saccade2
'Fix Up - T1/T2 Up - T1/T2 Down - Fix Down - Allow Saccades   As WAIT2 gets longer, the targets come down before saccade allowed

'These break down basically to:
'Learn to saccade
'Learn to hold until release signal before saccade
'Learn to double saccade with second target revealed after first saccade
'Learn to double saccade with second target shown overlapping --> simultaneously in time
'Learn to saccade to missing targets

'1) Saccade to T1 anytime (SR 1 [for holding until T1 up], full reward S1)
'2) Saccade to T1 after fixpt release (SR 1 [fade], SR 2 [hold until fixpt release], full reward S1)
'3) T2a up after saccade to T1 (release req??; SR 1/2 [fade], SR 3 [for S1], full reward S2)
'4) T2a/b up after saccade to T1 (fade in T2b?) (SR 1/2 should be gone by now, SR 3 [fade])
'5) T2a/b up before fix down (delay to fix down could start at 0) (full task to present targets)
'6) T1 + T2a/b contrast down before fix down 
'7) T1 + T2a/b down before fix down = full task
'Full task

'This does not create a JAWS problem because we will *never* JAWS during training.

'OK, we're changing the task AGAIN and not only that but we've got two different scenarios.  And the training may
'be significantly harder.  So let me outline the new new.
'
'We want the presence of a stimulus in the JAWS field to effectively be the saccade trigger.  The previous designs
'would have the JAWS field stimulus off of the screen before the saccade trigger, which kind of makes things iffy
'when we're relying on V1 layer-5 activity to be triggering the saccade (so that we can silence the V1 layer-5
'activity at the level of the pulvinar and hopefully disrupt efference copy of the saccade command).  But of course
'we need to disrupt the efference copy of the FIRST saccade to see the effect in the SECOND.  This brought Henry to
'suggest an inversion of the presentation order, where we first present the T2(s) and then subsequently have the T1
'in the JAWS field as a stimulus that is 1) being discriminated and 2) drives the S1.  As such, we have two designs
'we are choosing between: 1) Two T2s of orthogonal orientation followed by a T1, S1 to T1, S2 to the T2 orientation
'match; 2) One T2 followed by two T1s of orthogonal orientation (one in the JAWS field, one the other hemifield), 
'S1 to T1 orientation match, S2 to T2.  We're having a lot of trouble deciding on which of these designs to go with
'so I'm guessing I have to make both.
'
'It's not a huge deal to switch up some of these variables, and the task design (and even the full dialog) has not
'yet been completed, so that's no biggie.  The issue is going to be the training.  I had a nice training design and
'we're going to have to do something completely different.  I worry about having an intermediate saccade introduced
'into the training scheme.  I think we have to teach S1, then teach S1-S2, with only one stimulus (T2 or T1,
'depending on the task) then introduce the second stimulus and the contingency.  This probably looks at least somewhat 
'different for the two tasks.
'
'Task 1 (two T2s, one T1)
'1) Saccade to T1 (could be left or right hemifield, one is in JAWS field, use two orthogonal orientations)
'2) After S1, introduce iso-ori T2 only (with two possible locations) and give full reward for S2
'3) Fade in ortho-ori T2
'   At this point the animal is doing a two-saccade task with T1 determining which T2, all stimuli on screen
'4) Timeshift T2s so they appear at same time as T1, then keep going so they appear before T1 
'5) Begin fade-out of stimuli when S1 initiated (alternately may have T1 duration timed?)
'   We may want the fixpt to go off when the T1 goes up, from the very beginning, to encourage an immediate S1,
'   the only issue here is if that might encourage an express saccade.
'
'I think that would train Task 1.
'
'Task 2 (two T1s, one T2)
'1) Saccade to a single T1 (could be left or right hemifield, one is in JAWS field, use two orthogonal orientations)
'2) After S1, introduce iso-ori T2 only (one location) and give full reward for S2
'3) Fade in ortho-ori T1 opposite to iso-ori T1
'   At this point the animal is doing a two-saccade task with T2 determining which T1, all stimuli on screen
'4) Timeshift T2s so they appear at same time as T1, then keep going so they appear before T1 
'5) Begin fade-out of stimuli when S1 initiated (alternately may have T1 duration timed?)
'   We may want the fixpt to go off when the T1 goes up, from the very beginning, to encourage an immediate S1,
'   the only issue here is if that might encourage an express saccade.
'
'Honestly, the training is about the same.  Really the only difference is that in step 2 we have either one or
'two possible locations for the T2, and in step 3 we fade in a stimulus at a different location (T2 compete vs.
'T1 compete).  
'
'Fucking we might change this again.  MICROSTIM.  Henry says that there's only one thing that is KNOWN to
'generate a saccade from V1, and that's microstim in V1 layer-5.  And we're just HOPING that our other tasks
'will generate a saccade from V1.  So...duh.  We microstim.  We do the one thing that we KNOW will make this
'happen.  And block the efference copy in pulvinar via JAWS.  So the idea would be to up/down a saccade target - 
'and it doesn't really matter what it is - but to hit the animal with a microstim in the JAWS field conicident
'with the release of the fixation point on half the trials.  You would have four trial types: microstim on/off
'and JAWS on/off.  offM/offJ should just be a normal saccade; offM/onJ should produce a normal saccade (we 
'would hope!); onM/offJ should allow the animal to make a correct saccade after accounting for the "unexpected"
'saccade generated from V1 layer-5 via efference copy; onM/onJ should result in the animal making an incorrect 
'saccade, as if the "unexpected" saccade didn't happen, because the efference copy of that saccade is lost.
'
'Like this is the steelman experiment.  1) We KNOW the S1 was generated in V1 layer-5;  2) The primary efference
'copy is almost certainly gonig through pulvinar;  3) Nowhere else in the brain even KNOWS that the saccade is
'going to happen until it happens so there aren't even any higher-level motor commands pending that correspond to
'this saccade.
'
'So how so we train this?  
'
'1) Saccade to T1 (multiple locations?)
'2) Introduce microstim on some trials, T1 stays visible but fixpt goes off (well, super tricky design says
'   maybe fixpt reappears under microstim saccade, if that's reliable enough!)
'3) Fade out T1, hope animal can still do task under microstim conditions
'
'The idea of jumping the fixation point while the microsaccade happens is hilarious.  If those saccades are reliable
'and repeatable and you can slip the fixpt under, they will reacquire as if nothing ever happened!  But then I doubt
'they would know where to saccade...I don't think that's a good idea.
'
'Anyway, if we decide super-tricky is actually super-dumb, and it probably is, the final form of the task is easy
'in terms of VSG because we should be able to play a movie - everything turns off when the fixpt goes down, we can
'just stop the movie.  But the fade during training is going to be hard, I think.  We have to bring down a stimulus,
'advance to a new stimulus, and put it back up.  Iffffffy.
'
'
'
'And noooow, for the yet-another next experiment, we've got Yet Another Next Experiment Going First!  In this version
'of the experiment, we will be doing a single-saccade paradigm.  In the full version of the task, there will be:
'1) Fixation up
'2) Single Grating presented (JF or opposite hemisphere; fix up) 
'3) Gap (fix up)
'4) Two gratings presented (JF and opposite hemisphere; fix up)
'The S2 grating which appears in the same location as the S1 is the target; the S2 grating in the opposite location
'is a distractor; target and distractor are always 90 degrees different in orientation.  If the S2 target is a
'different orientation than S1, the animal will saccade to the target location.  If not, the animal will No-Go.
'A saccade to the distractor location is never rewarded.
'Training method on this is TBD, still puzzling through it.  I think we want to have both Go and No-Go from the jump?
'Or at least very very early.  Training the No-Go case is easiest, because it's just fixation then reward, but maybe 
'there's a case to be made to teach saccade first and then No-Go.  Maybe overthinking...start at full contrast, S1
'and target-loc S2.  Present either 2-sec S1 (reward after stim off = no-go), or 1-sec S1 and 1-sec S2 (allow saccade
'at orientation change and reward for saccade).  Then introduce gap.  Then fade in completely irrelevant S2 distractor.



'Serendipitously, the biggest U.S. hit for the band Chicago was a single titled "Look Away" ("If you see me walking
'by/And the tears are in my eye/Look away, baby look away") so we have a function name.  Well, that's something.


'I think we really kind of want this program to be a "Chicago Suite" that will allow us to do *any* of the Chicago
'style experiments.  But that would basically depend on being able to re-use variables that do "the same thing" and
'to reuse idle loop states that do "the same thing" so we need to plot out both of these...probably Excel will be
'better for this...thinking more about the idle states and maybe we want to have separate experimental "arcs" for
'each individual experiment, as it's going to be difficult to tightly map the various experiments to each other.
'In this experiment, we are going to have to use a "page advance" system, where on each trial we will construct
'multiple pages and then advance through them at appropriate times in the trial sequence. (Note that this is distinct
'from the normal "stimulus advance", which we will also use to go from trial to trial.)  Dan has not yet implemented
'this system, but I don't think it will be very hard for him. But my bottom line is that what these page advances
'correspond to will be very different based on experiment type, and there will be opportunities for opto, and for
'microstim in one experiment, and we'll have to have very different criteria and sampletexting and we should just 
'go for separate experimental arcs in the idle loop.




'Just as a note, the number of trials is growing and I want to keep an eye on it.  Assuming we do 50/50 splits of
'everything, we have:
'1) Loc1Visible/Drop (this changes to one or two saccades - now not sure that the one-saccade reduced task is even on the table)
'2) Loc1To/AwayJF
'3) Opto/No
'
'No idea whether we would want to block opto trials or to randomly distribute them
'
'Reading the grant section again, we may be varying Loc1 a little within the "JAWS field" to prevent anticipation of
'its location - I don't know how big the JAWS field will be but the small area dialog may be useful here to jitter
'things
'
'Gonna kill the small reward, not sure what it was needed for...well, I think the small reward stuff is for training
'If so, could use small reward on fixation hold and/or saccade to target 1
'
'Going to keep the small grid stuff, because it may be useful for "jittering" the Location 1 targets





